WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:11.460
Validation asks whether a detection recognizes its intended condition, avoids unrelated activity, produces useful context, and supports a safe response.

2
00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:19.560
In OT, validation methods must not create uncontrolled traffic, commands, configuration changes, or process effects.

3
00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:22.640
Start with low-risk methods.

4
00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:33.060
Review logic against known records, replay approved captured data in an isolated environment, and check field mappings, time handling, thresholds, and exclusions.

5
00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:38.760
A representative lab or vendor-supported test system can evaluate behavior that should not be generated in production.

6
00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:43.520
Tabletop review is valuable when technical reproduction is unsafe.

7
00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:52.660
Security analysts, operators, and engineers can walk through a scenario, expected telemetry, alert wording, triage questions, and response authority.

8
00:00:52.760 --> 00:01:00.040
Engineering review can uncover process conditions or maintenance patterns that the detection designer missed.

9
00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:06.000
Production validation should favor passive observation and carefully approved change windows.

10
00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:13.780
Do not send unplanned probes, controller operations, or disruptive protocol sequences merely to prove an alert fires.

11
00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:19.480
The validation plan should state safeguards, owners, rollback, and stop conditions.

12
00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:22.480
Tuning improves alert quality after deployment.

13
00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:31.300
Track false positives, duplicate alerts, missing context, data gaps, analyst effort, and whether the condition led to a useful decision.

14
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:38.280
Adjust thresholds and enrichment with evidence; do not silence broad categories just because they are inconvenient.

15
00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:44.800
Record test cases, results, reviewer approval, known limitations, and the detection version.

16
00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:50.340
Revalidate after parser, architecture, asset, or data-source changes.

17
00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:57.320
A detection is ready when its behavior is understood and operationally useful, not simply when the query runs without error.
